Ultra suspend, production: hrdl's configuration, validated on this device.

Promoted from bench quarantine to the primary kernel on 2026-08-08, on the
operator's direction, after R12 answered the question the quarantine
existed to ask.  Three rails-off ultra suspends on this exact stack: resume
on the RTC backstop (slept=60s), resume on the power button (slept=28s),
and a 40-minute alarm window measured at 4.64 mA -- against deep's ~20 mA.
Touch, Wi-Fi, pen and the display all recovered; the operator verified the
panel and touch on glass.  Full record:
doc/artifacts/pinenote-ultra-r12-20260808/ (and -r11-, and
-handshake-20260807 for how the wrong half of this configuration fails).

THE PAYLOAD IS A MATCHED PAIR AND MUST NEVER BE SPLIT:

  1. rockchip,suspend-state-override = <5> -- every suspend is ultra.
  2. vcca_1v8_pmu / vdda_0v9_pmu / vcc_3v3_pmu regulator-off-in-suspend,
     and sdmmc1 keep-power-in-suspend -> cap-power-off-card.

Either half alone is a proven-broken configuration:
  * override WITHOUT rails (R10/R11): firmware arms GPIO0 wake, parks at
    sram2wfi, and nothing -- not the RTC, not the power button -- ever
    wakes it.  Only a forced power-off exits.
  * rails WITHOUT override (rails-off + mem): never run by anyone;
    hrdl's DT makes it structurally unreachable and ours now does too.
Wake under this configuration is PMIC-mediated: the rk817 wakes on its
internal alarm or PWRON and restores the rails; the kernel genuinely
resumes (hrdl commit ee2c553f78: the override "is needed for the system
to resume").  vcc_3v3_pmu powers the GPIO0 pad bank, so every SoC-side
GPIO wake source is dead during suspend BY DESIGN; the PMIC paths (RTC
alarm, power button, charger) are the wake sources.

Consequences carried deliberately:
  * Wi-Fi/BT die across suspend (vcca_1v8_pmu feeds VCCIO4);
    cap-power-off-card makes the mmc core re-init the card on resume --
    proven working R12 A1.
  * The cyttsp5 touch controller loses vdd (fed by vcc_3v3_pmu) and can
    time out its first HID power command on resume; the cyttsp5 hunk
    below resets it and continues (hrdl's [HACK] 693fee19, adapted;
    their startup call is commented out in the original and this carries
    the same known-incomplete recovery).  Proven on glass R12.
  * vdda_0v9_pmu has zero DT consumers (SoC PMU-domain analog); killed
    because the working configuration kills it.

The suspend gates pin this payload exactly (inspect-pinenote-suspend-
gates.sh); validate-ultra-coupling.sh enforces the matched pair.

Origin: https://git.sr.ht/~hrdl/linux v6.19_ultra_suspend, commits
ee2c553f78 (DTS) and 693fee19 (cyttsp5), regenerated against our patched
7.0.11 tree.  The override property is accepted by our OF parser as of
the companion bsp-sip-probe change (exactly <5>, all other values
rejected at parse).

diff -uNr a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi	2026-08-08 13:32:11.491717819 -0600
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi	2026-08-08 16:23:41.045192929 -0600
@@ -26,6 +26,19 @@
 		rockchip,sleep-mode-config = <0x5ec>;
 		rockchip,wakeup-config = <0x10>;
 		rockchip,sleep-debug-en = <0x00>;
+		/*
+		 * Every suspend is ultra (LINUX_PM_STATE = 5).  This property
+		 * and the three regulator-off-in-suspend flips in this same
+		 * patch are a MATCHED PAIR and must never be separated:
+		 * rails-off + mem has never been run by anyone, and
+		 * override-without-rails is the R10/R11 configuration, proven
+		 * unwakeable (doc/artifacts/pinenote-ultra-handshake-20260807,
+		 * -r11-20260808).  Together they are hrdl's working
+		 * configuration, validated on this device 2026-08-08: three
+		 * resumes (RTC backstop, power button, 40-min alarm), 4.64 mA
+		 * measured (doc/artifacts/pinenote-ultra-r12-20260808).
+		 */
+		rockchip,suspend-state-override = <5>;
 	};
 
 	/*
@@ -449,7 +462,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -471,7 +484,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <900000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -504,7 +517,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -894,7 +907,7 @@
 	bus-width = <4>;
 	cap-sd-highspeed;
 	cap-sdio-irq;
-	keep-power-in-suspend;
+	cap-power-off-card;
 	mmc-pwrseq = <&sdio_pwrseq>;
 	non-removable;
 	pinctrl-0 = <&sdmmc1_bus4>, <&sdmmc1_clk>, <&sdmmc1_cmd>;
diff -uNr a/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c	2026-08-08 13:32:11.491774847 -0600
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c	2026-08-08 13:32:11.512433689 -0600
@@ -956,11 +956,30 @@
 static int __maybe_unused cyttsp5_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct cyttsp5 *ts = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int error = 0;
 
-	if (!device_may_wakeup(dev))
-		cyttsp5_power_control(ts, true);
+	if (!device_may_wakeup(dev)) {
+		error = cyttsp5_power_control(ts, true);
 
-	return 0;
+		/*
+		 * Under ultra suspend vcc_3v3_pmu is off, which takes this
+		 * controller's vdd with it, so the chip comes back cold and
+		 * the power-on handshake can fail.  Reset it and carry on
+		 * rather than propagating the error and losing touch for the
+		 * rest of the session.  Adapted from hrdl's [HACK] commit
+		 * 693fee19 -- their startup call is left commented out there
+		 * too, so this is a known-incomplete recovery.
+		 */
+		if (error) {
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(ts->reset_gpio, 1);
+			msleep(10);
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(ts->reset_gpio, 0);
+			msleep(20);
+			error = 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return error;
 }
 
 static SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(cyttsp5_pm, cyttsp5_suspend, cyttsp5_resume);
